# The container runtime the image carries: containerd and its runc shim, built from source at the
# commit that tag v2.4.2 of the public repository is, and runc, built at the commit that tag v1.5.3
# is. The commit, not the tag, is the pin: build.sh fetches that object and fails unless HEAD is
# it, and a tag can be moved.
#
# Why these releases (chosen 2026-11-13, from the repositories' tags and their GitHub releases):
# containerd v2.4 is the newest minor with a stable release, and v2.4.2, published 2026-10-13, 31
# days before that date, is its latest patch. runc v1.5.3, published 2026-11-12, is the newest
# stable release; runc is pinned to the newest because a runtime's fixes are security fixes.
#
# runc is linked against libseccomp, which build.sh builds static from the release tarball below.
# The digest is the tarball's line in the SHA256SUM file the release publishes beside it,
# https://github.com/seccomp/libseccomp/releases/download/v2.6.1/libseccomp-2.6.1.tar.gz.SHA256SUM;
# the version and the digest move together, in one pull request, which a reviewer checks against
# that file. GO_TOOLCHAIN is not here: build.sh takes it from containerd's go.mod, and builds runc
# with it too (runc's go.mod asks for an older one).
#
# Moved by: the PM, in a pull request that reruns `make image` and `make image-repro` and the QEMU
# test that starts a Pod, when a patch release of either is at least two weeks old or an advisory
# is published for a pinned one. No bot proposes it.
CONTAINERD_REPO=https://github.com/containerd/containerd
CONTAINERD_VERSION=v2.4.2
CONTAINERD_COMMIT=b7a25261e029d68978bbf290df0a62fc819b3c51
RUNC_REPO=https://github.com/opencontainers/runc
RUNC_VERSION=1.5.3
RUNC_COMMIT=46365c62967c76a765a5e347f48f0c7b3b571fe9
LIBSECCOMP_VERSION=2.6.1
LIBSECCOMP_URL=https://github.com/seccomp/libseccomp/releases/download/v2.6.1/libseccomp-2.6.1.tar.gz
LIBSECCOMP_SHA256=501f66c667225d53791b97e1d7cf85ab764c297d04881f60f38f451c4b0ee1be
# libseccomp's build generates its syscall tables with gperf, which the host may not have, so
# build.sh builds gperf from this release too. GNU publishes a detached signature and no digest
# file: the digest below is the tarball's, checked on 2026-11-13 against
# https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gperf/gperf-3.3.tar.gz.sig with GNU's keyring (Bruno Haible's key,
# E0FF BD97 5397 F77A 32AB 76EC B630 1D9E 1BBE AC08). The tool runs at build time only.
GPERF_VERSION=3.3
GPERF_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gperf/gperf-3.3.tar.gz
GPERF_SHA256=fd87e0aba7e43ae054837afd6cd4db03a3f2693deb3619085e6ed9d8d9604ad8
